        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<BR>
With the development of International Space Station (ISS) and longer duration space missions, astronauts will need antibiotic therapy to treat various bacterial infections.  Quinolones are effective agents for gastrointestinal and respiratory tract infections.  Microgravity induced changes in the absorption of orally administered medications may influence systemic drug availability and therefore influence the therapeutic efficacy/toxicity of the drug. 
<P>
Specific Aims:
<LI> To quantitatively assess whether the time interval between moxifloxacin and an aluminum hydroxide/magnesium hydroxide antacid mixture (Maalox) or between moxifloxacin and the antidiarrheal agent bismuth subsalicylate (Pepto-Bismol) oral administrations can affect fluoroquinolone antibiotic absorption kinetics in a tail-suspended microgravity rat model.
<LI> To study the effect of microgravity on the gastrointestinal (GI) absorption kinetics of the model water-insoluble and inherently poorly absorbed drug griseofulvin using a tail-suspended microgravity rat model.
<LI> To assess whether simulated weightlessness can affect the disposition kinetics of antipyrine, a marker compound for hepatic oxidative metabolism, and whether gender differences exist. 
<P>
APPROACH:<BR>
Oral absorption characteristics of four quiolone antibiotics will be studied in an animal model using an analog to microgravity.
<P>
RESULTS:<BR>
Co-administration of moxifloxacin and Maalox to control or tail-suspended (TS) rats markedly impaired the rate and extent (bioavailability) of moxifloxacin absorption, even when the drug administration was separated by 60 minutes. However, in TS rats, the degree of this drug-drug interaction was significantly reduced. Co-administration of moxifloxacin with Pepto-Bismol to control rats markedly impaired the rate but not the extent of moxifloxacin absorption. In striking contrast, there was no significant effect on either the rate or extent of moxifloxacin absorption in TS rats. 
<p>
Simulated weightlessness appreciably and significantly altered several griseofulvin pharmacokinetic parameters. The mean terminal half-lives after oral administration of griseofulvin to control or TS rats were significantly longer [4.30 h (P=0.011) for control rats and 6.13 h (P=0.014) for TS rats] than the value observed after intravenous griseofulvin administration. This suggests that griseofulvin exhibits flip-flop kinetics after oral administration. The mean AUC values observed after oral administration of griseofulvin to control and TS rats, dose normalized from 50 mg/kg to 5 mg/kg, were significantly smaller (P<0.02) than the mean value observed after a 5 mg/kg intravenous dose of griseofulvin. This reflects the poor oral absorption characteristics of the drug. The mean Cmax value for griseofulvin in tail-suspended rats was significantly higher than the value observed in control rats. This suggests that simulated weightlessness can appreciably and significantly alter the GI absorption characteristics of a poorly water soluble drug. 
<p>
Simulated weightlessness had statistically significant and marked effects on antipyrine pharmacokinetics after intravenous administration. The mean AUC was increased by 81%; the mean terminal elimination half-life was prolonged by 74%; the mean total body clearance was decreased by 45%; and the mean residence time was increased by 58%.  No significant difference existed between the two rat groups with respect to the mean apparent volume of distribution. The results obtained in the present investigation are consistent with the significant decreases in total hepatic cytochrome P-450 content observed in rats aboard both the Cosmos 1887 space flight and Skylab 3 space station. The data from antipyrine study suggest that hepatic oxidative enzymatic function is significantly reduced under simulated weightlessness conditions in male rats, but not in female rats. 
<p>
